FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/formatter.rs:415 — `Statement::Try` with `catch_var: None` and non-empty `catch_body` (bare `catch` with no name, which parser.rs:981-989 explicitly allows by making the identifier optional) emits nothing: the `if let Some(var)` has no else branch, so the entire catch body is silently dropped — `try / say 1 / catch / say 2 / end` formats to `try / say 1 / end`, breaking idempotence and losslessness — concrete fix: emit the `catch` line when `catch_var.is_some() || !catch_body.is_empty()`, writing the name only when present.
2. [MAJOR] src/formatter.rs:780 — `open_line(line)` claims only the comment on `stmt.span.line`, but a block opening can span two lines: parser.rs:607-608 does `skip_newlines()` before expecting `Then`, so `if 1 is 1` / `then // note` is valid with the note on line 2 while `stmt.span.line` is line 1; the note is then emitted as a leading comment on the first body statement instead of trailing the opening — concrete fix: claim the trailing comment on the `then`/continuation line (track the opening's end line) or add a test pinning split openings.
REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
